{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document (PRD): Error Boundary Implementation\n\n## Problem\nCurrently, in the internal ops dashboard, an uncaught runtime error within any React component crashes the entire React application tree. When this happens, users lose access to all features, including navigation and the `src/components/Header.jsx` bar, requiring a full browser refresh and interrupting operational workflows.\n\n## Goals\n- Prevent isolated component crashes from taking down the entire dashboard application.\n- Provide a generic, graceful fallback UI when a component render failure occurs.\n- Preserve the global layout (specifically the `src/components/Header.jsx` and primary navigation) so operators can continue using unaffected parts of the application.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Component-specific customized fallback views (a generic fallback is sufficient for initial delivery).\n- Integration with remote telemetry or third-party error monitoring platforms (errors will be caught and rendered locally for now).\n- Catching non-rendering errors (e.g., background asynchronous promise rejections or event handlers) that do not unmount the React component tree.\n\n## User Impact\n- Operators will no longer face a blank white screen when an isolated sub-component fails.\n- Navigation remains accessible, allowing users to move to unaffected pages or reload specific views without losing overall application state.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- **[PRODUCT_DECISION]** At what level should error boundaries be placed to isolate crashes (e.g., wrapping individual page/route content while preserving `src/components/Header.jsx`, or wrapping specific sub-component widgets)?\n- **[PRODUCT_DECISION]** Should the fallback UI offer a user recovery mechanism (such as a 'Try Again' button or resetting error state upon route navigation) rather than requiring a full browser refresh?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document (TRD): Error Boundary Implementation\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **React Class Component Requirement**: React 18 (`package.json`) requires a class component implementing `getDerivedStateFromError` and/or `componentDidCatch` to function as an Error Boundary, as functional components cannot act as error boundaries without custom class wrappers or the `react-error-boundary` package.\n- **Placement Strategy**: In `src/App.jsx`, placing a single boundary at the root level will cause errors to unmount `src/components/Header.jsx`. Boundaries should be positioned below global navigation or at the route-level container to isolate failures.\n- **Routing Integration**: `react-router-dom` (^6.20.0) is present in `package.json`. React Router v6 offers route-level `errorElement` handling for router loaders and actions, which operates alongside standard React component lifecycle boundaries.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Asynchronous & API Errors**: Errors originating inside `src/api/client.js` or asynchronous callbacks/promises are not caught by standard React error boundaries unless explicitly caught and propagated into React render state.\n- **State Persistence**: Without an automated reset mechanism (e.g., listening to route transitions) or manual recovery trigger ('Retry'), the boundary remains in an error state until an entire page reload occurs.\n- **Full Tree Unmounting**: Care must be taken in `src/App.jsx` so that sub-component rendering errors do not bubble past page-level boundaries to tear down the entire DOM.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- **[ENGINEERING_DECISION]** Should the error boundary be implemented as a custom React class component, or should a third-party dependency like `react-error-boundary` be added to `package.json`?\n- **[ENGINEERING_DECISION]** Should error handling integrate with React Router v6's route-level `errorElement`, or rely solely on React lifecycle error boundaries inside `src/App.jsx`?\n- **[ENGINEERING_DECISION]** Should asynchronous and API errors originating from `src/api/client.js` be routed into React error boundaries, or kept within localized state/promise handling?\n\n## Estimate\n- 2–3 days (up to 8 days worst case depending on placement granularity and router integration)."}